## Product Mental Model

Figma Make should understand Spotler Engage as a platform where incoming conversations, outgoing publishing, media monitoring, and team workflows come together.

The core mental model is:

1. Connect channels
   - Social platforms, messaging channels, WhatsApp, live chat, and chatbots are connected.

2. Centralise conversations
   - Comments, direct messages, reviews, WhatsApp messages, and live chat enquiries arrive in one central inbox.

3. Organise
   - Conversations are grouped into configurable inbox views, tagged, prioritised, assigned, and given statuses.

4. Collaborate
   - Team members use ownership, assignments, notes, permissions, and workflows to avoid duplicate work and ensure the right person responds.

5. Respond
   - Agents answer messages from the same environment, with conversation history and channel context.

6. Publish
   - Teams create, schedule, publish, edit, and manage social posts from one shared overview.

7. Monitor
   - Structured queries track online mentions across news, blogs, and social platforms.

8. Coordinate during peaks or crises
   - Monitoring, publishing, and webcare work together to identify urgent issues, prioritise messages, and maintain consistent communication.

9. Report
   - Teams use workload, response, conversation, and performance insights to improve efficiency and service quality.

Do not treat Spotler Engage as only a social media scheduler, only a helpdesk inbox, or only a monitoring tool. It combines webcare, social messaging, publishing, monitoring, WhatsApp, mobile access, and collaboration.

---

## Main Product Areas and Capabilities

### Central Inbox and Webcare

Spotler Engage brings incoming messages from multiple channels into one central inbox.

Incoming conversation types may include:

- Social media comments
- Private messages
- Reviews
- WhatsApp messages
- Live chat conversations
- Chatbot handovers
- Direct messages
- Public interactions

Core inbox capabilities:

- Configurable inbox views
- Assignment
- Tags
- Conversation status
- Ownership
- Internal notes
- Conversation history
- Prioritisation
- Team collaboration

Design implication:

- The inbox is not just a message list. It is an operational workspace for high-volume webcare.
- Users need to know what is new, what is assigned, what is urgent, what is waiting, and who owns each conversation.
- Avoid designing messages as isolated objects without status, channel, ownership, or workflow context.

---

### Conversation Management

Spotler Engage helps teams process conversations efficiently and consistently.

Conversation management includes:

- Opening and replying to messages
- Assigning conversations to team members
- Adding internal notes
- Adding tags
- Updating status
- Tracking ownership
- Seeing channel context
- Reviewing conversation history
- Escalating sensitive issues
- Avoiding duplicate responses

Design implication:

- Conversation state and ownership must be clear.
- Internal collaboration should be separated from public replies.
- Teams need confidence that no one else is already handling the same conversation.

---

### Automatic Routing and Smart Workflows

Spotler Engage supports smart workflows and automatic routing to reduce manual work.

Workflow concepts may include:

- Routing conversations to the right team
- Prioritising urgent messages
- Assigning based on channel, topic, tag, or workload
- Supporting peak moments
- Reducing duplicate handling
- Tracking message status through the process

Design implication:

- Routing should support accountability and speed.
- Users should understand why a conversation appears in a specific inbox or assignment queue.
- Avoid black-box routing where ownership is unclear.

---

### Social Publishing and Scheduling

Spotler Engage supports creating, scheduling, publishing, editing, and managing posts for connected social channels.

Supported publishing channels include:

- Facebook
- Instagram
- Threads
- X / Twitter
- LinkedIn
- TikTok

Publishing capabilities:

- Create posts
- Schedule posts
- Manage posts centrally
- Edit posts
- Use a shared overview
- Optimise posts with an AI assistant
- Coordinate communication during campaigns or crises

Design implication:

- Publishing should be connected to webcare and monitoring.
- Social content may create follow-up interactions that need to be handled in the inbox.
- Shared overview and governance matter, especially for teams.

---

### Monitoring and Listening

Spotler Engage supports monitoring online mentions through structured search queries.

Monitoring sources may include:

- News
- Blogs
- Social platforms

Monitoring capabilities:

- Build search queries
- Manage queries
- Refine queries with filters and keywords
- Use AI-assisted query building
- Track brand mentions
- Identify trend signals
- Support crisis communication
- Support reputation management

Design implication:

- Monitoring is about early signals and reputation insight, not only analytics.
- Query building should be understandable and refinable.
- Monitoring should connect to webcare and publishing during sensitive situations.

---

### Crisis and Reputation Management

Spotler Engage supports teams during incidents, peak loads, policy changes, public disruptions, or reputationally sensitive moments.

Crisis and reputation workflows may include:

- Detecting increased message volume
- Monitoring mentions
- Identifying urgent or sensitive messages
- Prioritising conversations
- Assigning work across teams
- Coordinating public responses
- Publishing updates
- Maintaining consistency
- Reporting on response performance

Design implication:

- Crisis workflows need speed, overview, priority, and governance.
- Monitoring, publishing, and webcare should not feel disconnected.
- Designs should support pressure, volume, urgency, and risk.

---

### WhatsApp Messaging

Spotler Engage supports WhatsApp as part of the same webcare and communication environment.

WhatsApp capabilities:

- Receive WhatsApp messages
- Reply to WhatsApp conversations
- Set up WhatsApp campaigns
- Manage WhatsApp alongside social channels
- Retain conversation history per contact

Design implication:

- WhatsApp should not feel like a separate standalone tool.
- Conversation history and channel context matter.
- WhatsApp may be used for one-to-one service and campaign messaging.

---

### Live Chat and Chatbots

Spotler Engage can connect other communication channels such as live chat and chatbots.

Capabilities may include:

- Website live chat via widget
- Automated chatbot conversations
- Chatbot handover to human agents
- Central handling alongside other channels

Design implication:

- Chat and chatbot conversations should fit into the same assignment, status, and ownership model as other conversations.
- Avoid treating chat as disconnected from webcare workflows.

---

### Mobile Access and Progressive Web App

Spotler Engage offers mobile access through a progressive web app.

Mobile use cases:

- Publish messages on the go
- Answer webcare enquiries from any location
- Handle urgent messages outside the office
- Support teams during events or incidents

Design implication:

- Mobile access supports continuous availability.
- Mobile workflows should prioritise speed, clarity, and safe action.
- Avoid assuming webcare always happens from a desktop office environment.

---

### Reporting and Performance Insight

Spotler Engage provides insight into webcare workload, response speed, conversations, and service performance.

Reporting may include:

- Response times
- Conversation volume
- Workload
- Team performance
- Channel performance
- Message status
- Assignment status
- Service performance
- Peak moments
- Monitoring signals
- Publishing activity

Design implication:

- Reporting should help teams improve efficiency, collaboration, and customer satisfaction.
- Workload insight matters for team leads.
- Response-time reporting matters for service quality.

