## Feature Design Guidance

### Design for high-volume conversation handling

Spotler Engage is built for teams handling many conversations across many channels.

Good designs should support:

- Fast triage
- Clear ownership
- Assignment
- Status tracking
- Tags
- Priority
- Channel visibility
- Internal notes
- Workload overview

Avoid:

- Treating messages as simple unread notifications
- Hiding who owns a conversation
- Making users switch context to understand channel or status

---

### Keep public replies and internal collaboration separate

Agents need to collaborate privately while responding publicly.

Good designs should clearly distinguish:

- Public replies
- Internal notes
- Assignments
- Status changes
- Tags
- System actions

Avoid any ambiguity that could lead to an internal note being sent as a public response.

---

### Connect monitoring, publishing, and webcare

Spotler Engage is strongest when monitoring, publishing, and webcare support each other.

Good designs should show relationships between:

- Mentions
- Incoming conversations
- Published posts
- Campaigns
- Crisis topics
- Response coordination

Avoid designing these areas as completely unrelated tools.

---

### Design for crisis and peak moments

During crisis or peak loads, teams need overview and control.

Good designs should support:

- Urgency
- Volume changes
- Priority messages
- Assignment at scale
- Consistent responses
- Monitoring signals
- Publishing updates
- Team workload

Avoid workflows that only work in calm, low-volume situations.

---

### Make channel context visible

Each channel has different expectations and constraints.

Good designs should show:

- Source channel
- Message type
- Public or private context
- Conversation history
- Channel-specific actions
- Customer-facing vs internal actions

Avoid flattening all channels so much that important context is lost.

---

### Make mobile use practical

The progressive web app supports publishing and webcare on the go.

Good mobile designs should prioritise:

- Assigned conversations
- Urgent messages
- Quick replies
- Safe publishing
- Status updates
- Clear ownership

Avoid forcing complex desktop workflows into mobile screens without prioritisation.

---

### Make reporting operational

Reports should help teams improve webcare performance and collaboration.

Useful reporting angles:

- Response time
- Workload
- Channel volume
- Conversation status
- Assignment distribution
- Peak periods
- Team performance
- Monitoring trends

Avoid dashboards that do not help teams decide where to improve.

---

## Important Assumptions to Preserve

Preserve these assumptions when generating product concepts, UX flows, screens, or feature ideas:

- Spotler Engage is an integrated webcare and social messaging platform.
- Conversations from multiple channels are handled in one central inbox.
- Users need configurable inbox views, assignment, tags, notes, ownership, and statuses.
- Webcare, publishing, and monitoring are connected parts of the same operation.
- The product supports social publishing and scheduling.
- The product supports monitoring and listening through structured queries.
- The product supports WhatsApp conversations and campaigns.
- The product supports live chat and chatbots.
- The product supports mobile use through a progressive web app.
- High-volume and peak-load situations are important.
- Crisis and reputation management are important use cases.
- Teams need speed, overview, consistency, and accountability.
- Reporting should support operational improvement.
- Multiple team members can work simultaneously.
- Role-based access and permission structures matter.
- Media monitoring is available for organisations in the Netherlands.

---

## Things Figma Make Should Avoid

Avoid these mistakes when designing Spotler Engage features:

- Do not treat Engage as only a social media scheduler.
- Do not treat Engage as only a helpdesk ticketing tool.
- Do not treat Engage as only a monitoring dashboard.
- Do not replace “conversation” with “ticket” unless specifically appropriate.
- Do not ignore assignment, ownership, status, tags, or internal notes.
- Do not hide the channel a message came from.
- Do not mix internal notes and public replies.
- Do not design webcare without team collaboration.
- Do not design publishing without considering follow-up interactions.
- Do not design monitoring without connecting it to reputation or crisis action.
- Do not assume all usage happens during normal low-volume operations.
- Do not ignore crisis, peak load, or sensitive situations.
- Do not assume WhatsApp is separate from the main inbox.
- Do not ignore mobile access through the progressive web app.
- Do not invent unrelated SaaS objects such as projects, boards, or generic tasks unless specifically needed.
- Do not replace product terminology with vague terms like “items” or “records”.
- Do not overfocus on visual styling; that belongs in the Design System Skill.

---

## Edge Cases and Product Logic to Consider

### Duplicate handling risk

Multiple team members may see the same conversation. Designs should make ownership and assignment clear to prevent duplicate replies.

### Internal note accidentally sent publicly

Internal collaboration must be visually and functionally separated from public replies.

### Message volume spike

During incidents, campaigns, outages, policy changes, or crises, message volume can increase quickly. Designs should support triage, routing, and priority.

### Sensitive or urgent issue

A message may require escalation, careful wording, or coordination with communications leadership.

### Conversation spans multiple messages

A customer may send several messages in a row or across channels. Conversation history helps maintain continuity.

### Channel-specific constraints

Different channels may support different actions, formats, or expectations. Designs should not assume every channel behaves the same.

### WhatsApp conversation history

WhatsApp interactions should retain conversation history per contact and should be manageable alongside social webcare.

### Chatbot handover

A chatbot may handle part of a conversation before handing over to a human agent. Designs should preserve handover context.

### Monitoring query too broad

A query may capture too many irrelevant mentions. Users need filters, keywords, and refinement.

### Monitoring query too narrow

A query may miss important mentions. AI-assisted query building can help improve coverage.

### Publishing during a crisis

Posts may need coordination, approval, or careful timing during sensitive situations.

### Mobile response risk

Mobile users may act quickly under pressure. Designs should make reply, note, status, and publish actions clear and safe.

### Permission differences

Not every user can publish, assign, monitor, or configure settings. Role-based access should be considered.

---

## Relationship to the Separate Design System Skill

This Skill provides product context only.

Use this Skill for:

- Product behaviour
- Workflows
- Webcare terminology
- User roles
- Conversation logic
- Inbox and assignment logic
- Publishing logic
- Monitoring logic
- WhatsApp and messaging context
- Crisis and reputation management context
- Mobile and progressive web app context
- Reporting and operational insight
- Feature-specific product assumptions

Do not use this Skill for:

- Component styling
- Typography
- Colour usage
- Layout systems
- Spacing
- Icons
- Button styles
- Form styling
- Navigation visual patterns
- Design tokens

For those topics, use the separate Spotler Design System Skill or attached Design System documentation.

When both Skills are available:

- Use this Skill to decide what the feature should do and how it should behave.
- Use the Design System Skill to decide how the feature should look and be constructed visually.

---

## Final Checklist for Figma Make

Before generating or redesigning a Spotler Engage feature, check:

- Does the design use Spotler Engage terminology?
- Is the relevant user role clear?
- Is the feature grounded in webcare, social messaging, publishing, monitoring, WhatsApp, live chat, chatbots, mobile access, or reporting?
- Does the design avoid generic SaaS assumptions?
- Does it make channel context visible?
- Does it clearly show assignment, ownership, status, tags, and internal notes where relevant?
- Does it separate internal collaboration from public replies?
- Does it support team collaboration and simultaneous work?
- Does it account for high-volume and peak-load situations?
- Does it support crisis and reputation management where relevant?
- Does it connect monitoring, publishing, and webcare where appropriate?
- Does it make WhatsApp part of the same operational environment?
- Does it account for mobile use through the progressive web app?
- Does reporting help teams improve response speed, workload, and service quality?
- Does it avoid visual styling decisions that belong to the Design System Skill?

